The '''Eurovision Song Contest 1975''' was the 20th edition of the [[Eurovision Song Contest]], held on 22 March 1975 at the {{lang|sv|[[Stockholm International Fairs|Stockholmsmässan]]|i=unset}} in [[Stockholm]], Sweden, and presented by [[Karin Falck]]. It was organised by the [[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) and host broadcaster {{lang|sv|[[Sveriges Radio]]|i=unset}} (SR), who staged the event after winning the {{escyr|1974||1974 contest}} for {{esccnty|Sweden|y=1974}} with the song "[[Waterloo (song)|Waterloo]]" by [[ABBA]].

Broadcasters from nineteen countries were represented at the contest{{snd}}a new record number of participants. {{esccnty|Turkey}} made its first entry in the contest, and {{esccnty|France}} and {{esccnty|Malta}} returned after a one- and two-year absence, respectively. {{esccnty|Greece}}, after participating for the first time in the previous year's event, opted not to participate in 1975, due to the Turkish invasion of Cyprus in 1974.

The winner was the {{esccnty|Netherlands|y=1975}} with the song "[[Ding-a-dong]]", composed by [[Dick Bakker]], written by {{ill|Will Luikinga|nl}} and [[Eddy Ouwens]], and performed by the group [[Teach-In (band)|Teach-In]]. This was the Netherlands' fourth contest victory, matching the record number of contest wins previously set by France and {{esccnty|Luxembourg}}. Having been the opening song of the contest, it was also the first time that a country had won from first position in the running order. The {{esccnty|United Kingdom|y=1975}}, {{esccnty|Italy|y=1975}}, {{esccnty|France|y=1975}}, and {{esccnty|Luxembourg|y=1975}} rounded out the top five, with the United Kingdom achieving a record-extending ninth second-place finish.

A [[Voting at the Eurovision Song Contest|new voting system]] was introduced at this contest; each country gave 12 points to its favourite, 10 points to its second favourite, and then 8 points to 1 point to other countries in descending order of preference. This numerical order of awarded points has since been used in every subsequent edition of the contest.

== Location ==
[[File:Älvsjö - KMB - 16001000290784.jpg|thumb|250px|left|{{lang|sv|[[Stockholm International Fairs|Stockholmsmässan]]|i=unset}}, Stockholm – host venue of the 1975 contest]]
The 1975 contest took place in [[Stockholm]], Sweden, following the country's victory at the {{escyr|1974||1974 contest}} with the song "[[Waterloo (song)|Waterloo]]" performed by [[ABBA]]. It was the first time that Sweden had hosted the event.<ref>{{cite web |title=Sweden – Participation history |url=https://www.eurovision.com/eurovision-song-contest/countries/sweden |publisher=European Broadcasting Union (EBU) |access-date=3 May 2026}}</ref><ref name="recall" /> The chosen venue was the {{lang|sv|[[Stockholm International Fairs|Sankt Eriks-Mässan]]|i=unset}}, an exhibition centre in the [[Älvsjö]] district of southern Stockholm opened in 1971; in 1976 the venue was renamed to {{lang|sv|Stockholmsmässan|i=unset}}.<ref name="recall" /><ref>{{cite web |title=Stockholmsmässan – Our history |url=https://www.stockholmsmassan.se/about-us/in-business-since-1942 |publisher=[[Stockholm International Fairs|Stockholmsmässan]] |access-date=10 April 2024 |language=en}} {{Dead link|date=January 2025|fix-attempted=yes |url=}}</ref>

The Swedish broadcaster {{lang|sv|[[Sveriges Radio]]|i=unset}} (SR) had initially been reluctant to stage the event, mainly due to the high costs that came with it which would have been placed on the organisation. There had also been considerable pressure and disquiet from [[Left-wing politics|left-wing groups]] in the country that initially opposed the amount of money being spent by the public broadcaster on a commercial event, which subsequently developed into a wider protest against the general commercialisation of [[Music of Sweden|music in Sweden]];<ref name="recall" /><ref>{{cite web |title=All eyes on Stockholm |url=https://eurovision.tv/story/all-eyes-on-stockholm |publisher=European Broadcasting Union (EBU) |access-date=10 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171017110728/https://eurovision.tv/story/recalling-sweden-s-first-staging-of-the-contest-in-1975 |archive-date=17 October 2017 |language=en |date=4 January 2016 |url-status=live}}</ref> this led to street protests and a counter-festival, {{lang|sv|{{ill|Alternativfestivalen|sv}}}} ({{gloss|the Alternative Festival}}), being held during the week of Eurovision 1975.<ref name="SR">{{cite news |title=Swedish protests against hosting Eurovision Song Contest |url=https://sverigesradio.se/artikel/6194571 |access-date=10 April 2024 |publisher={{lang|sv|[[Sveriges Radio]]|i=unset}} (SR) |date=1 July 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230114224645/https://sverigesradio.se/artikel/6194571 |archive-date=14 January 2023 |language=en |url-status=live}}</ref>{{sfn|Thorsson|Verhage|2006|pp=118–119}}{{sfn|West|2020|pp=91–94|loc="1975"}}<ref>{{cite web |title=Alternativfestivalen |trans-title=The Alternative Festival |url=https://mellopedia.svt.se/index.php/Alternativfestivalen |work=Mellopedia |publisher=[[Sveriges Television]] (SVT) |access-date=10 April 2024}}</ref>

SR had attempted to negotiate with the [[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) and other participating broadcasters to enact a form of cost-sharing to fund the event, however a solution failed to materialise prior to the contest and SR was ultimately faced with funding the contest alone. These discussions, however, did eventually lead to the introduction of a new financing system for {{escyr|1976}} and future events, with the running costs of the event being split across all participating countries.<ref name="recall" />{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=180–182}} However, fears of the potential costs required to host the event should Sweden have won again, coupled with the pressure from left-wing groups, meant that SR ultimately decided not to participate in the 1976 event.<ref name="recall" /><ref name="SR" />{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|p=227}}

== Participants ==
