{{ESC 1975 participants}}
[[File:Ellen Nikolaysen (11097025886).jpg|thumb|upright|[[Ellen Nikolaysen]] represented {{esccnty|Norway}} for a second time]]
A total of 19 countries participated in the 1975 contest{{snd}}a new record number of participants. This included the first ever appearance of {{esccnty|Turkey}}, and entries from {{esccnty|France}} and {{esccnty|Malta}}, which last participated in {{escyr|1973}} and {{escyr|1972}}, respectively.<ref name="recall">{{Cite web |title=Recalling Sweden's first staging of the contest in 1975 |date=22 March 2013 |url=https://eurovision.tv/story/recalling-sweden-s-first-staging-of-the-contest-in-1975 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171017110728/https://eurovision.tv/story/recalling-sweden-s-first-staging-of-the-contest-in-1975 |archive-date=17 October 2017 |access-date=10 April 2024 |publisher=European Broadcasting Union (EBU)}}</ref><ref name="ESC1975">{{Cite web |title=Stockholm 1975 |url=https://eurovision.tv/event/stockholm-1975 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531111909/https://eurovision.tv/event/stockholm-1975 |archive-date=31 May 2022 |access-date=10 April 2024 |publisher=European Broadcasting Union (EBU)}}</ref> Broadcasters in {{esccnty|Greece}}, which participated for the first time in {{escyr|1974}}, and {{esccnty|Austria}}, last seen in the contest in 1972, had also considered participating in the contest, however no entries from these countries were ultimately submitted;{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=180–182}}<ref name="ESC1975" /> Greece had reportedly decided against participating at a late stage, and may have opted to refuse to compete alongside Turkey following the [[Turkish invasion of Cyprus]].{{sfn|Thorsson|Verhage|2006|pp=118–119}}{{sfn|West|2020|pp=91–94|loc="1975"}}{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=180–182}}

[[Ellen Nikolaysen]] competed in the contest for a second time, having previously participated for {{esccnty|Norway|y=1973|t=Norway in 1973}} as a member of the [[Bendik Singers]].{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=182–189}} Additionally, [[John Farrar]], a member of [[the Shadows]], had previously supported the {{esccnty|United Kingdom|y=1973|t=United Kingdom in 1973}} as a [[backing vocalist]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Nissim |first1=Mayer |title=When The Shadows represented the UK at Eurovision without Cliff Richard |url=https://www.goldradiouk.com/news/music/shadows-eurovision-song-contest/ |publisher=[[Gold (British radio network)|Gold]] |access-date=10 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230514192007/https://www.goldradiouk.com/news/music/shadows-eurovision-song-contest/ |archive-date=14 May 2023 |date=12 May 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>

