}}
The contest was held on 22 March 1975, beginning at 21:00 ([[Central European Time|CET]]) and lasting 2 hours and 12 minutes.<ref name="ESC1975" />{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=182–189}} The contest was presented by the Swedish television presenter, producer and director [[Karin Falck]].<ref name="recall" /><ref>{{cite web |title=Karin Falck |url=https://mellopedia.svt.se/index.php/Karin_Falck |work=Mellopedia |publisher=[[Sveriges Television]] (SVT) |access-date=10 April 2024 |language=sv}}</ref> Following the confirmation of the 19 participating countries, a draw was held in [[Geneva]] on 24 January 1975 to determine the running order (R/O) of the contest.{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=180–182}}

The contest was opened by a film montage portraying various cultural stereotypes of Sweden and the [[Swedes|Swedish people]]. Each entry was preceded by a video postcard, which served as an introduction to that country's entry and to create a transition between entries to allow stage crew to make changes on stage;<ref>{{cite web |last1=Egan |first1=John |title=All Kinds of Everything: a history of Eurovision Postcards |url=https://escinsight.com/2015/05/22/all-kinds-of-everything-a-history-of-eurovision-postcards/ |publisher=ESC Insight |access-date=24 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150524073150/http://www.escinsight.com/2015/05/22/all-kinds-of-everything-a-history-of-eurovision-postcards/ |archive-date=24 May 2015 |date=22 May 2015 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Kurris |first1=Denis |title=Eurovision 2022: The theme of this year's Eurovision postcards |url=https://www.esc-plus.com/eurovision-2022-the-theme-of-this-years-eurovision-postcards/ |publisher=ESC Plus |access-date=24 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220501103821/https://www.esc-plus.com/eurovision-2022-the-theme-of-this-years-eurovision-postcards/ |archive-date=1 May 2022 |date=1 May 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> the postcards showed each country's entrant backstage painting a portrait of themselves and the flag of their nation onto a blank canvas.<ref name="contestAV" />{{sfn|O'Connor|2010|pp=60–63}} The interval act was entitled "The World of [[John Bauer (illustrator)|John Bauer]]" ({{langx|sv|John Bauers värld}}), comprising a montage of examples of the Swedish illustrator's work, particularly from his anthology ''[[Among Gnomes and Trolls]]'', set to music from the orchestra.<ref name="contestAV">{{Cite AV media |title=Eurovision Song Contest 1975 |date=22 March 1975 |type=Television programme |language=en, fr, sv |publisher=[[Sveriges Radio]] (SR) |location=[[Stockholm]], Sweden}}</ref>{{sfn|O'Connor|2010|p=217}} The medallions awarded to the winning songwriters were presented by the Secretary-General of the European Broadcasting Union {{ill|Henrik Hahr|sv}}.<ref name="contestAV" />{{sfn|O'Connor|2010|p=217}}

The winner was the {{esccnty|Netherlands|y=1975}} represented by the song "[[Ding-a-dong]]", composed by Bakker, written by {{ill|Will Luikinga|nl}} and [[Eddy Ouwens]], and performed by [[Teach-In (band)|Teach-In]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Stockholm 1975 – Teach-In |url=https://eurovision.tv/participant/teach-in |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221006055734/https://eurovision.tv/participant/teach-in |archive-date=6 October 2022 |access-date=11 April 2024 |publisher=European Broadcasting Union (EBU)}}</ref> It was the Netherlands' fourth contest win, following victories in {{escyr|1957}}, {{escyr|1959}} and {{escyr|1969}}; the Netherlands thus joined {{esccnty|France}} and {{esccnty|Luxembourg}} as the countries with the most contest wins at that point.{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=189–193}}<ref>{{Cite web |title=Netherlands – Participation history |url=https://www.eurovision.com/eurovision-song-contest/countries/netherlands |publisher=[[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) |access-date=3 May 2026}}</ref> It was additionally the first time that the song which was performed first had gone on to win the contest.<ref name="recall" />{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=189–193}} The UK came second for a record-extending ninth time, and Malta, which had come last in its two previous contest appearances, achieved its best result to date with a twelfth-place finish.{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=189–193}}<ref>{{cite web |title=Malta – Participation history |url=https://www.eurovision.com/eurovision-song-contest/countries/malta |publisher=[[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) |access-date=3 May 2026}}</ref> Turkey, meanwhile, finished in last place on its debut appearance.<ref>{{cite web |title=Türkiye – Participation history |url=https://www.eurovision.com/eurovision-song-contest/countries/turkiye |publisher=[[European Broadcasting Union]] (EBU) |access-date=3 May 2026}}</ref>

