== Broadcasts<span class="anchor" id="Broadcasters and commentators"></span> ==
<!-- Anchor in the header is the old section name -->
Broadcasters competing in the event were required to relay the contest via its networks; non-participating EBU member broadcasters were also able to relay the contest. Broadcasters were able to send commentators to provide coverage of the contest in their own native language and to relay information about the artists and songs to their television viewers.<ref name="Rules" />

In addition to the participating nations, the contest was also reportedly aired, live or deferred, by broadcasters in Eastern European countries via [[International Radio and Television Organisation|Intervision]], and in Australia, Denmark, Greece, Hong Kong, Iceland, Japan, Jordan, Morocco and South Korea.<ref name="recall" />{{sfn|Roxburgh|2014|pp=180–182}}<ref name="Aragón">{{cite news |title=Grandes medidas de seguridad |trans-title=Great security measures |url=https://www.zaragoza.es/hemeroteca/prensa/HMZ_P0041/HMZ_P0041_1975-03-21/HMZ_P0041_1975-03-21.pdf |access-date=18 December 2024 |work=Aragón expres |date=21 March 1975 |page=8 |location=[[Zaragoza]], Spain |language=es |via={{ill|Ayuntamiento de Zaragoza|es}}}}</ref> The contest was reported to have had a possible maximum audience of over 700 million people.<ref name="VrijeVolk">{{cite news |last=Van Dijk |first=Ale |title=Songfestival-vesting |trans-title=Eurovision fortress |url=https://www.delpher.nl/nl/kranten/view?coll=ddd&identifier=ddd:010958723:mpeg21:p009 |access-date=11 April 2024 |work=[[Het Vrije Volk]] |date=22 March 1975 |page=9 |location=[[Rotterdam]], Netherlands |language=nl |via=[[Delpher]]}}</ref>

A planned broadcast in Chile by its public broadcaster {{lang|es|[[Televisión Nacional de Chile]]|i=unset}} was prevented by SR, following pressure from the [[Swedish Musicians' Union]] in opposition to the [[Military dictatorship of Chile|Chilean military dictatorship]].<ref name="HS" /><ref>{{cite news |last=Van Dijk |first=Ale |title=Zweedse tv: geen beelden van songfestival naar Chili |trans-title=Swedish TV: no footage from the Eurovision Song Contest to Chile |url=https://resolver.kb.nl/resolve?urn=ddd:010958723:mpeg21:p001 |access-date=9 January 2023 |work=[[Het Vrije Volk]] |date=22 March 1975 |page=1 |location=[[Rotterdam]], Netherlands |language=nl |via=[[Delpher]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=22 March 1975 |title='Affaire' Eurovisión probó solidaridad de la TV chilena |trans-title=Eurovision 'affair' tested solidarity of Chilean TV |language=es |work=[[El Mercurio]] |location=[[Santiago]], Chile |pages=33}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.commoditiescontrol.com/commodity-market/dowjonescommoditiesnews/geopolitics-of-eurovision-chile-edition-20150505DN008017.html |title=Geopolitics of Eurovision: Chile Edition |date=5 May 2015 |access-date=6 May 2017 |work=CommoditiesControl |archive-date=17 October 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171017222625/http://www.commoditiescontrol.com/commodity-market/dowjonescommoditiesnews/geopolitics-of-eurovision-chile-edition-20150505DN008017.html |url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Rolf Rembe]], spokesman for the union, said that broadcasting the festival to Chile "would give the impression that relations between Chile and world artists are normal".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NpJcAAAAIBAJ&dq=eurovision+1975+chile&pg=PA15&article_id=1116,1821192 |title=El certamen de Eurovisión no llegará a Chile |trans-title=The Eurovision contest will not come to Chile |work=[[La Opinión]] |location=[[Los Angeles|Los Angeles, CA]], United States |date=23 March 1975 |access-date=15 May 2024 |page=7 |language=es |via=[[Google Books]]}}</ref>

